TI  - The interactive effects of low testosterone and sleep insufficiency on cognitive impairment: Evidence from the NHANES 2011-2014
T2  - PSYCHIATRY RESEARCH
AB  - Objective This study investigates the associations between low testosterone levels, sleep insufficiency, and cognitive impairment in adults aged >= 60 years. Methods Serum testosterone levels were measured using liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry, and sleep duration was categorized as insufficient (<7 h) or normal (>= 7 h). Cognitive function was assessed using the Consortium to Establish a Registry for Alzheimer's Disease Battery, which includes the immediate word list learning (CERAD-WL) and delayed recall (CERAD-DR) Test, the Animal Fluency Test (AFT), and the Digit Symbol Substitution Test (DSST). Results Participants with low testosterone levels (<300 ng/dL) exhibited significantly higher odds of cognitive impairment with odds ratios (ORs) for the CERAD test, AFT, and DSST were 1.55 (95 % CI: 1.27-1.90), 1.50 (95 % CI: 1.29-1.81), and 1.29 (95 % CI: 1.03-1.61), respectively. Sleep insufficiency was also significantly associated with cognitive impairment, with ORs for CERAD, AFT, and DSST of 1.23 (95 % CI: 1.00-1.52), 1.26 (95 % CI: 1.03-1.89), and 1.33 (95 % CI: 1.07-1.67), respectively. RCS analysis revealed a nonlinear relationship, with a marked increase in cognitive risk below a testosterone threshold of approximately 300 ng/dL. Subgroup analysis revealed a synergistic effect between low testosterone and sleep insufficiency, with participants exposed to both factors showing the highest odds of cognitive impairment. The ORs for cognitive impairment were 2.10 (95 % CI: 1.65-2.65) for CERAD test, 2.14 (95 % CI: 1.72-2.66) for AFT, and 1.85 (95 % CI: 1.42-2.42) for DSST. Mendelian randomization analysis provided evidence confirming a significant causal relationship. Conclusion Low testosterone and insufficient sleep are independently associated with cognitive impairment in older adults, and their combined effects exacerbate cognitive decline.
